[gd_scene load_steps=46 format=3]

; Tri-axle tipper / dump semi-trailer. Hand-built low-poly (Kenney ships no trailers at all), flat
; boxes and 8/10-segment cylinders to match the kit's flat-shaded look.
;
; AUTHORED WITH THE ORIGIN AT THE KINGPIN, ground at y = -1.05 — the family convention, measured
; off the tractor unit's fifth-wheel plate. See flatbed.tscn's header for why.
;
; THE ONLY TRAILER WITH A FUNCTION. tipper.gd owns what it consumes (the chassis PTO and a
; proportional hydraulic valve) and what the tip does to the load; this file owns the steel:
;
;   - TipBody is a PIVOT AT THE REAR HINGE, at (0, -0.06, 6.25), and everything that tips is a
;     child of it. So the hinge line is the node's own origin and the pose is one rotation about
;     local X — the same "the datum IS the origin" discipline the kingpin gets.
;   - The body is 5.15 m long and starts at z 1.10, BEHIND the tractor's tail (z 0.96 in this
;     frame). The gooseneck ahead of it stays exposed, which is what a real bulk tipper looks like
;     and is also what keeps the gooseneck rule: nothing hangs below the coupling plane (y = 0)
;     over the tractor.
;   - THE RAMS ARE POSED FROM MARKERS, not from constants: RamAnchorL/R are the frame eyes and
;     TipBody/RamHeadL/R the body eyes, and tipper.gd lays each ram between its pair and slides the
;     rod out of the barrel. Re-author either marker and the ram follows. They span 1.12 m closed
;     and 3.12 m at full tip, which is why the rod is a separate mesh rather than a stretched
;     cylinder — a hydraulic ram extends, it does not stretch.
;   - The TAILGATE is TOP-HINGED (Tailgate is its hinge line, at the top rear of the body) and
;     swings under the load's own weight once the body is up. It is not separately commanded, so
;     there is no second control and no second signal.
;
; COLLISION DOES NOT FOLLOW THE TIP — IT IS TWO AUTHORED POSES AND ONE SWAP. A CollisionShape3D
; re-transformed every tick inside a RigidBody3D rebuilds the compound and re-derives the inertia
; tensor at 60 Hz, on the one body that is also writing its own centre of mass — two churning
; physics properties on a jointed body is exactly how a rig starts buzzing. So CollisionTipBody is
; the LOWERED pose and CollisionTipBodyRaised is the SAME BoxShape3D placed at the full-tip pose
; (the lowered box swept 42 deg about the hinge at (0, -0.06, 6.25), which is where the arithmetic
; in its transform comes from), and tipper.gd enables exactly one of them, switching at mid-travel.
; Two rebuilds a tip instead of sixty a second. Nothing interpolates: between the two poses the
; collision is whichever end the body is nearer, which is wrong by at most half a tip and only
; while the rams are actually moving — and the interlock means that is a rig standing still.
; The raised box exists because the interlock refuses the RAISE DIRECTION ONLY (rolling away with
; the body up must hold it, not command it down onto whatever is under it), so 5.2 m of body in the
; air is a pose you really can drive, and it has to hit the bridge it looks like it should hit.
;
; MEASURED against the tractor unit: 1.92 over the frame, body 1.80 wide x 0.95 deep x 5.15 long,
; sides topping out at y 0.99 = 2.04 m above the road parked, and about 5.4 m at full tip.
;
; Swing clearance: the frontmost part is the gooseneck at 0.33 m ahead of the kingpin and 0.96
; half-width, swinging on 1.02 m inside the 1.15 m to the cab's rear face. The tipping body starts
; well behind the kingpin, so tipping only ever moves it further away. test_trailer sweeps it.

